lessons.body:
## The problem
"Design the Facebook or Twitter news feed for the web." A user opens the page, sees posts, scrolls for a long time, likes and comments, and sees new posts arrive. It is the most common frontend system design prompt because it touches pagination, rendering performance, caching, optimistic updates and accessibility at once.

## Requirements
**Functional:** view a feed of posts (text, images, video), infinite scroll, create a post, like and comment, see a "new posts" indicator.

**Non-functional:** fast first load (good LCP) on mobile, smooth scrolling after hundreds of posts, works on flaky networks, accessible to screen readers and keyboard users, i18n-ready.

**Out of scope (say it):** ranking algorithm, ads, stories, DMs.

## Architecture
```text
FeedPage (route, SSR shell for first page)
 +-- Composer ---------------------> createPost()
 +-- NewPostsBanner  <------------- live channel (SSE): "3 new posts"
 +-- FeedList (virtualised)
 |     +-- PostCard x N
 |     |     +-- MediaGrid (lazy images, video poster)
 |     |     +-- ActionBar (like, comment, share) -> toggleLike()
 |     |     +-- CommentPreview
 |     +-- Sentinel (IntersectionObserver) -> fetchNextPage()
 +-- data layer: query cache + normalised entities + mutation queue
```

The server renders the first page of posts in HTML (fast LCP, no spinner); the client hydrates and takes over pagination. The data layer owns all fetching; `PostCard` only reads from the store and dispatches intents.

## Data model
```ts
interface User { id: string; name: string; handle: string; avatarUrl: string }
interface Media {
  id: string;
  kind: "image" | "video";
  url: string;
  width: number;            // needed to reserve space and avoid layout shift
  height: number;
  blurhash?: string;        // tiny placeholder
}
interface Post {
  id: string;
  authorId: string;
  text: string;
  media: Media[];
  likeCount: number;
  commentCount: number;
  likedByMe: boolean;
  createdAt: string;
}
interface Store {
  users: Record<string, User>;
  posts: Record<string, Post>;
  feed: { ids: string[]; nextCursor: string | null; pendingNewIds: string[] };
}
```

**Normalised** means each entity is stored once by id and lists hold ids. If the same post appears in the feed and in a modal, liking it updates both. If a user changes their avatar, every post updates.

## Interface
```ts
// GET /api/feed?cursor=<opaque>&limit=10
interface FeedPage {
  posts: Post[];
  users: Record<string, User>;
  nextCursor: string | null;
}

// POST /api/posts            { text, mediaIds, clientId }
// PUT  /api/posts/:id/like   (idempotent)   DELETE /api/posts/:id/like
// GET  /api/feed/stream      SSE: { type: "new_posts", count: number, newestId: string }
```

### Why cursor pagination
With offset pagination (`?page=3`), new posts at the top shift every item down, so page 3 repeats items you already saw; deleting shifts them up and you skip items. A **cursor** is an opaque pointer to the last item seen ("posts older than id X / timestamp T"), so the next page is stable no matter what is inserted above. It is also cheaper for the database (index seek instead of scanning and discarding OFFSET rows).

## Infinite scroll with IntersectionObserver
Do not listen to `scroll` and measure; observe a sentinel element near the bottom.

```tsx
function useInfiniteScroll(onHit: () => void, enabled: boolean) {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLDivElement | null>(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    const el = ref.current;
    if (!el || !enabled) return;
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      ([entry]) => entry.isIntersecting && onHit(),
      { rootMargin: "800px 0px" }   // start loading well before the end
    );
    io.observe(el);
    return () => io.disconnect();
  }, [onHit, enabled]);
  return ref;
}
```

Guard against double fetches (`enabled = !isFetching && nextCursor !== null`). Keep a visible "Load more" button as a fallback for keyboard users and when the observer misfires; infinite scroll also makes the footer unreachable, so put footer links elsewhere.

## List virtualisation
After 500 posts with images, the DOM holds tens of thousands of nodes; memory and style recalculation make scrolling janky. **Virtualisation** renders only the posts in (and slightly beyond) the viewport and uses spacers for the rest.

```text
 [ top spacer: height of posts 0..39 ]
 [ PostCard 40 ]  <- overscan
 [ PostCard 41 ]  <- visible
 [ PostCard 42 ]  <- visible
 [ PostCard 43 ]  <- overscan
 [ bottom spacer: estimated height of 44..N ]
```

Feed posts have variable height, so the virtualiser must measure rows after render (TanStack Virtual's `measureElement`, react-virtuoso). Trade-offs: browser find-in-page cannot see unrendered posts, scroll position restoration needs care, and screen readers only see rendered items. A cheaper first step is CSS `content-visibility: auto` with `contain-intrinsic-size`, which skips rendering work for off-screen posts while keeping them in the DOM.

## Optimistic likes and comments
The user expects the heart to fill instantly. Update the store first, send the request, and roll back on failure.

```ts
async function toggleLike(postId: string) {
  const prev = store.posts[postId];
  const next = { ...prev, likedByMe: !prev.likedByMe,
    likeCount: prev.likeCount + (prev.likedByMe ? -1 : 1) };
  store.setPost(next);                                  // instant UI
  try {
    const res = await (next.likedByMe ? api.like(postId) : api.unlike(postId));
    store.setPost({ ...store.posts[postId], likeCount: res.likeCount }); // reconcile with server truth
  } catch {
    store.setPost(prev);                                // rollback
    toast("Couldn't like the post. Try again.");
  }
}
```

Use idempotent PUT/DELETE so retries are safe. For comments, insert a pending comment with a client-generated id and a "sending" style, then swap in the server id when it returns, or mark it "failed, tap to retry".

## New-post banner
Do not insert new posts at the top while the user is reading: the content jumps under their finger. Receive "new posts available" over SSE (or poll every 30-60 s), store the ids in `pendingNewIds`, show a "Show 3 new posts" pill, and on click prepend them and scroll to top. This is what Twitter/X and Facebook do.

## Media
- Reserve space with known `width`/`height` (or CSS `aspect-ratio`) so images do not cause CLS.
- `loading="lazy"` for off-screen images, but **not** for the first post's image (it is often the LCP element; give it `fetchpriority="high"`).
- `srcset`/`sizes` so phones download small images; AVIF/WebP via a CDN.
- Videos: show a poster, autoplay muted only when mostly visible (IntersectionObserver with threshold 0.5), pause when it scrolls away.

## Accessibility
The WAI-ARIA **feed pattern**: the list container has `role="feed"` with `aria-busy="true"` while loading more, and each post is an `article` with `aria-labelledby` (author) and `aria-posinset`/`aria-setsize` (use -1 if the size is unknown). Keyboard users need a way past an endless list (a "skip to composer" link, or Page Down moving between articles). Announce "10 new posts loaded" through a polite live region. Buttons for like need `aria-pressed`.

## Other optimisations
- **Prefetch** the next page when the user is a screen away; cancel if they navigate.
- **Stale-while-revalidate:** returning to the feed shows the cached list instantly, then refreshes.
- **Scroll restoration:** store the anchor post id and offset, not the pixel position (heights change).
- **Security:** post text is rendered as text, never as HTML; links are parsed into tokens and rendered with `rel="noopener noreferrer"`.
- **i18n:** relative time with `Intl.RelativeTimeFormat`, counts with compact `Intl.NumberFormat`.

## Common mistakes
- Offset pagination for a live feed.
- Scroll listeners with `getBoundingClientRect` on every event.
- Inserting new posts at the top while the user reads.
- Lazy-loading the LCP image.
- Denormalised copies of a post in several lists that drift out of sync.

## In the interview
Lead with requirements, sketch the tree, show the normalised store, justify cursor pagination, then go deep on scroll performance (observer + virtualisation), optimistic mutations and the feed a11y pattern. Expect follow-ups on how virtualisation handles variable heights and how you restore scroll position on back navigation.

## The problem
"Design a typeahead search box", as in Google search, an e-commerce search bar, or a "mention a user" picker. It looks small, but it tests network control (debounce, cancellation, races), caching, keyboard interaction and the ARIA combobox pattern. Interviewers often ask for a reusable component that different teams can plug data sources into.

## Requirements
**Functional:** as the user types, show up to N suggestions; select with mouse, touch or keyboard; highlight the matched text; optionally show recent searches when the input is empty.

**Non-functional:** results feel instant (under ~100-200 ms perceived), do not hammer the server, always show results for the **latest** query, fully keyboard and screen-reader accessible, works on mobile, configurable (data source, min chars, debounce, rendering of items).

## Architecture
```text
Autocomplete (controller: state machine)
 +-- Input (role=combobox)          onChange -> debounce -> query(q)
 +-- Listbox (role=listbox, popup)
 |     +-- Option x N (role=option, highlighted match)
 +-- LiveRegion ("5 results available")

query(q):
  cache.get(q) ? render now : fetcher(q, abortSignal) -> cache.set(q) -> render if q is still current
```

Make the component **headless-friendly**: a hook (`useAutocomplete`) owns state and ARIA props, and the consumer renders items. This is how Downshift and React Aria's `useComboBox` are built.

## Data model
```ts
interface Suggestion { id: string; label: string; kind?: "query" | "product" | "user"; meta?: string }

interface AutocompleteState {
  query: string;
  results: Suggestion[];
  status: "idle" | "loading" | "success" | "error";
  open: boolean;
  activeIndex: number;        // -1 means nothing highlighted
}

interface CacheEntry { results: Suggestion[]; expiresAt: number }
```

## Interface
```ts
// GET /api/suggest?q=iph&limit=8
interface SuggestResponse { query: string; results: Suggestion[] }

// Component API
interface AutocompleteProps {
  fetchSuggestions: (q: string, signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<Suggestion[]>;
  onSelect: (s: Suggestion) => void;
  minChars?: number;          // default 1-2
  debounceMs?: number;        // default ~200
  renderItem?: (s: Suggestion, query: string) => ReactNode;
}
```

Echoing `query` in the response lets the client discard stale responses even without cancellation.

## Debounce
Without debounce, typing "iphone" fires six requests. Debounce waits until the user pauses (150-300 ms) before sending. Throttle (at most one per interval) suits continuous events like scroll; debounce suits "act when they stop".

```ts
function useDebounced<T>(value: T, ms: number): T {
  const [v, setV] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const t = setTimeout(() => setV(value), ms);
    return () => clearTimeout(t);
  }, [value, ms]);
  return v;
}
```

## Race conditions and cancellation
Debounce reduces requests but does not fix ordering. If "ip" is slow and "iph" is fast, "ip" can arrive last and overwrite the correct results. Two defences, use both:

1. **Cancel** the previous request with `AbortController`.
2. **Ignore** any response whose query is not the current one.

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  if (debounced.length < minChars) return setResults([]);
  const cached = cache.get(debounced);
  if (cached) return setResults(cached);
  const ctrl = new AbortController();
  setStatus("loading");
  fetchSuggestions(debounced, ctrl.signal)
    .then((r) => { cache.set(debounced, r); setResults(r); setStatus("success"); })
    .catch((e) => { if (e.name !== "AbortError") setStatus("error"); });
  return () => ctrl.abort();      // the next keystroke aborts this request
}, [debounced]);
```

Aborting stops the browser waiting and frees the connection; the server may still do the work, which is why debounce matters too.

## Client cache: LRU with TTL
Users backspace and retype; cache results per query. Bound memory with an **LRU** (evict least recently used) and a **TTL** (suggestions go stale). A `Map` keeps insertion order, which makes a tiny LRU:

```ts
class LruCache<V> {
  private map = new Map<string, { v: V; exp: number }>();
  constructor(private max = 100, private ttlMs = 5 * 60_000) {}
  get(k: string): V | undefined {
    const e = this.map.get(k);
    if (!e) return;
    if (Date.now() > e.exp) { this.map.delete(k); return; }
    this.map.delete(k); this.map.set(k, e);       // mark as most recent
    return e.v;
  }
  set(k: string, v: V) {
    this.map.delete(k);
    this.map.set(k, { v, exp: Date.now() + this.ttlMs });
    if (this.map.size > this.max) this.map.delete(this.map.keys().next().value!);
  }
}
```

Bonus: for small, static datasets (countries, a team's users) skip the network and filter locally; you can also filter the cached "ip" results while "iph" is in flight to show something instantly. Normalise keys (trim, lower-case) so "IPhone " hits the cache.

## Keyboard navigation and the ARIA combobox
Follow the WAI-ARIA APG combobox pattern:

- Input: `role="combobox"`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls="listbox-id"`, `aria-autocomplete="list"`, and `aria-activedescendant` pointing at the highlighted option id.
- Popup: `role="listbox"`; items `role="option"` with `aria-selected` on the active one.
- **Focus stays in the input.** Arrow keys move `activeIndex`; `aria-activedescendant` tells screen readers which option is active.
- Keys: Down/Up move (wrap or clamp), Enter selects, Escape closes (second Escape clears), Tab closes without hijacking focus, Home/End optional.
- Announce result counts in a polite live region ("5 suggestions available"), debounced so it does not chatter.
- Use `onMouseDown` + `preventDefault` on options (or pointer events) so the input's blur does not close the list before the click lands.

## Highlighting matches safely
Split the label into matched and unmatched segments and render them as React text with a `mark` or bold span. Never build an HTML string and inject it: the label comes from the server and may contain user content. Escape regex special characters in the query before building a `RegExp`.

## Mobile
Use `type="search"` and `enterkeyhint="search"`, turn off `autocorrect`/`autocapitalize` where they fight the suggestions, make options at least 44px tall, and keep the list above the on-screen keyboard (the `visualViewport` API reports the visible area). On small screens a full-screen search overlay is often better than a dropdown.

## Rate limits and failures
Debounce, cache and minimum length are your client-side rate limiting. On HTTP 429 back off and keep the last good results visible. Show a quiet inline error rather than a toast for every keystroke. The server can return `Cache-Control: max-age=60` for popular prefixes so a CDN absorbs the traffic.

## Trade-offs
- Longer debounce means fewer requests but more lag; 150-300 ms is the usual range, shorter on fast backends.
- Prefetching popular prefixes or shipping a top-1000 list in the bundle makes first keystrokes instant but costs bytes.
- Server-side ranking and personalisation beat client filtering for large catalogues.

## Common mistakes
- Debounce without cancellation or a stale-response check.
- Moving DOM focus into the list, which breaks typing.
- No cache bound, so memory grows forever in a long session.
- Building highlighted HTML with string concatenation (XSS).
- Closing the popup on blur before the click on an option registers.

## In the interview
Draw the controller with input, listbox and cache. Walk through one keystroke end to end: debounce, cache lookup, abort previous, fetch, stale check, render, announce. Then show the ARIA attributes and the keyboard map. Expect follow-ups: "How would you make it reusable for other teams?" (headless hook, render props) and "What if the API is slow?" (local filtering of cached results, skeleton rows, keep previous results while loading).

deepDives.body:
A whiteboard has no central text sequence; it is a set of objects with properties. Users edit offline, and the server should stay simple. That favours a CRDT: replicas merge in any order, so offline edits and flaky networks need no transform logic on the server.

**Model shapes as nested maps, not blobs.**

```ts
const doc = new Y.Doc();
const shapes = doc.getMap<Y.Map<unknown>>("shapes");   // id -> shape props
const order = doc.getArray<string>("order");            // z-order

function addRect(id: string, x: number, y: number) {
  doc.transact(() => {
    const s = new Y.Map<unknown>();
    s.set("type", "rect"); s.set("x", x); s.set("y", y); s.set("w", 120); s.set("h", 80); s.set("fill", "#ffd166");
    shapes.set(id, s);
    order.push([id]);
  }, LOCAL_ORIGIN);
}
```

If a shape were one JSON value, Alice changing its colour and Bob moving it at the same time would be a conflict where one change is lost (last writer wins on the whole value). With a Y.Map per shape, each property is merged independently, so both edits survive.

**Z-order** lives in a Y.Array of ids. "Bring to front" is delete plus push; two users doing it concurrently still converge. Fractional index strings on each shape are an alternative that avoids moving array items.

**What the CRDT does not give you:** semantic validity (a shape deleted by one user while another resizes it: the delete wins, and the resize applies to a removed item), permissions (the server must drop updates from view-only tokens) and persistence (the server stores snapshots so new joiners do not replay the full history).

**Interview point:** Figma itself uses a server-authoritative model with last-writer-wins per property, which is conceptually close to this per-property map design.

**Undo** in a shared document must never revert someone else's work. Yjs's `UndoManager` tracks only transactions whose origin is in its `trackedOrigins` set, so tag every local change with a local origin and leave remote updates untracked.

```ts
const undo = new Y.UndoManager([shapes, order], {
  trackedOrigins: new Set([LOCAL_ORIGIN]),
  captureTimeout: 400,            // merge rapid changes into one step
});
window.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
  if ((e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey) && e.key === "z") e.shiftKey ? undo.redo() : undo.undo();
});
```

A drag produces dozens of position updates; wrapping the whole gesture in one `doc.transact` (or stopping capture at pointerup) makes it a single undo step. Undoing your move of a shape that someone else has since recoloured restores your position but keeps their colour, because the properties are separate map entries.

**Offline:** `y-indexeddb` persists every update locally. While offline, edits apply to the local doc and are stored. On reconnect, the provider exchanges state vectors with the server: each side sends only the updates the other lacks, and the CRDT merges them. Nothing special is needed in app code beyond showing the connection state.

**Edge cases to test and document:**
- Edit offline in two browsers, reconnect both, confirm identical output.
- Delete a shape offline while another user edits it online.
- Close the tab while offline and reopen: the local edits are still there and sync later.

**Storage:** call `navigator.storage.persist()` so the browser is less likely to evict the board under storage pressure.

Cursors, names, colours and current selections change constantly and have no long-term value. Storing them in the Y.Doc would bloat history and persist garbage. Yjs's **awareness** protocol is a separate, ephemeral state map per client, broadcast over the same provider and expired automatically when a client stops sending heartbeats.

```ts
const awareness = provider.awareness;
awareness.setLocalStateField("user", { name, color });

const sendCursor = throttle((x: number, y: number) => {
  awareness.setLocalStateField("cursor", { x, y });   // canvas coordinates, not screen pixels
}, 50);

awareness.on("change", () => markLiveLayerDirty());
```

**Coordinates:** send cursor positions in canvas (world) coordinates, then project with each viewer's own pan and zoom. Screen coordinates would put cursors in the wrong place for anyone with a different viewport.

**Smoothness:** at 20 updates per second, cursors look jumpy; interpolate between the last two positions on each animation frame for smooth motion without more network traffic.

**Selections:** broadcast selected shape ids so others see a coloured outline and avoid editing the same shape; this is social locking, not real locking.

**Privacy and cleanup:** remove local state on `beforeunload` (`awareness.setLocalState(null)`) so cursors disappear immediately; otherwise the timeout removes them after about 30 seconds.

**Accessibility:** a "people in this room" list with names is the accessible equivalent of floating cursors; announce joins and leaves in a polite live region, sparingly.

Formulas look simple but need operator precedence, unary minus, ranges, function calls and references. A **Pratt parser** (top-down operator precedence) handles this in about 150 lines and is easy to extend.

**Tokens:** numbers, strings, booleans, cell refs (`A1`, `$B$2`), ranges (`A1:B10`), identifiers (function names), operators (`+ - * / ^ &` and comparisons), parentheses and commas.

**AST:**

```ts
type Expr =
  | { kind: "num"; value: number }
  | { kind: "str"; value: string }
  | { kind: "ref"; row: number; col: number; absRow: boolean; absCol: boolean }
  | { kind: "range"; from: Ref; to: Ref }
  | { kind: "unary"; op: "-"; arg: Expr }
  | { kind: "binary"; op: "+" | "-" | "*" | "/" | "^" | "&" | "=" | "<" | ">"; left: Expr; right: Expr }
  | { kind: "call"; name: string; args: Expr[] };
```

**Precedence (low to high):** comparison, `&`, `+ -`, `* /`, `^`, unary minus. In a Pratt parser each operator has a binding power; `parseExpr(minBp)` keeps consuming infix operators whose power exceeds `minBp`. Exponent is right-associative, which you get by recursing with a slightly lower power for the right side.

**Evaluation:** a recursive `evaluate(expr, getValue)` where ranges evaluate to 2D arrays for functions like `SUM`. Errors are **values**, not exceptions: `#DIV/0!` propagates through arithmetic, and functions like `IFERROR` can catch it.

**Extracting dependencies:** walk the AST once after parsing to collect refs and ranges; that is the input to the dependency graph. Cache the AST per cell so recalculation never re-parses.

**Testing:** a table-driven Vitest suite (`["=1+2*3", 7]`, `["=-2^2", ...]`, `["=SUM(A1:A3)", ...]`) is the fastest way to build confidence and a great README artefact. Decide and document edge cases explicitly, such as how unary minus binds relative to `^` (spreadsheet apps differ from maths convention here).
